    "question": {
      ":question/id": "cae6dbe0-0c46-5e09-9f7c-0257c4dbc8cd",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following diagrams gives the slope field at the points indicated for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3-(-2)xy?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(0,0)$,$(2,-1)$,$(-1,-1)$.\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,0)}=3-(-2)(0)(0)=3, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(2,-1)}=3-(-2)(2)(-1)=-1, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1,-1)}=3-(-2)(-1)(-1)=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/repairs/2026-10-20-early-cohort/jobs/17592186172038/workspace/evidence/visual-repair/assets/1abee2902e8b64cf07d918a436d373485ad0086ae17f7c7bc84bd74afbdf5871.png)",
