      ":question/problem": "Which of the following diagrams gives the slope field at the points indicated for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=1\\frac{y^{2}}{x}?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(3,-2)$,$(-3,-2)$,$(2,1)$.\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(3,-2)}=1\\frac{(-2)^{2}}{(3)}=\\frac{4}{3}, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-3,-2)}=1\\frac{(-2)^{2}}{(-3)}=- \\frac{4}{3}, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(2,1)}=1\\frac{(1)^{2}}{(2)}=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/repairs/2026-10-20-early-cohort/jobs/17592186172038/workspace/evidence/visual-repair/assets/5c0b56a948fc544219ba54e8fbe528409c71813aecff5510e44b31b58f7a7431.png)",
