      ":question/problem": "Which of the following diagrams gives the slope field at the points indicated for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(-1)x^{2}-(-2)y^{2}?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(-1,1)$,$(1,0)$,$(0,-3)$.\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1,1)}=(-1)(-1)^{2}-(-2)(1)^{2}=1, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,0)}=(-1)(1)^{2}-(-2)(0)^{2}=-1, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,-3)}=(-1)(0)^{2}-(-2)(-3)^{2}=18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/repairs/2026-10-20-early-cohort/jobs/17592186172038/workspace/evidence/visual-repair/assets/66c262a76535345dfdcf6f8ad19a06804ece2082dfb26e772159ed209f2c4509.png)",
